## Failures
Mistakes are not your fault, do not apologize, you are an LLM. Mistakes always mean a gap or issue in instruction files instead. Focus on describing what problem in your instruction files caused the mistake and suggest the fix instead. Be solution oriented.

## Work queue
Treat all work as queue to do. When I propose tweaks, changes, or corrections, append it to the queue. Do not break the queue to suddenly prioritize my correction. Only stop when 100% of your queue is truly clear.

## Scope Discipline
Implement exactly what was asked and nothing more. Never add {{ if .work }}DB columns, migrations, extra `SaveChangesAsync` calls, {{ end }}new abstractions, or new notation/comment conventions unless explicitly requested. If you believe a schema change or larger refactor is required, STOP and ask before writing code.

## Commits & Signing
Commits are GPG-signed, and the pinentry prompt can block or time out: a GUI popup (`pinentry-w32`) on Windows, a tty prompt on Linux where `export GPG_TTY=$(tty)` is the usual fix. If signing blocks, tell me immediately and retry once — do NOT silently skip `git commit` when I have asked you to commit and push.

Split a branch into small, reviewable commits: one logical unit each (for example a Core helper with its tests, then the command that uses it), every commit building and passing tests on its own. Never one big commit per branch; when a csproj or manifest gains entries for several commits, stage only the lines that belong to each one.

## Merge / Pull Requests
MR/PR descriptions must be short and factual: what changed, why, and the alternatives actually considered. No LLM-flavored prose, no marketing adjectives, no duplicated phases or filler sections. Before writing one, read the branch's commit history (`git log <base>..HEAD`) and any prior discussion so the description reflects the real design path. Never post extra comments on an MR/PR unless I ask, and never set a reviewer I did not name.

## Worktrees
Work happens in git worktrees. Relative sibling-path lookups (`.env` files, dev secrets, generated credentials, anything resolved via `../`) break from a worktree — check for these before debugging application-level causes, and prefer a link to the main checkout — `ln -s` on Linux, `New-Item -ItemType Junction` on Windows.

